1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
In the last few weeks

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:04,640
we've been looking at various aspects of the social history of London,

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:09,280
and this morning we're continuing with a look at life in the area called the East End.

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:11,820
I'll start with a brief history of the district,

5
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:15,490
and then focus on life in the first half of the twentieth century.

6
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:20,692
Back in the first to the fourth centuries AD, when the Romans controlled England,

7
00:00:20,692 --> 00:00:26,800
London grew into a town of 45,000 people, and what's now the East End

8
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,124
The area by the river Thames,

9
00:00:28,124 --> 00:00:32,954
and along the road heading north-east from London to the coast-consisted of farmland

10
00:00:32,954 --> 00:00:36,670
with crops and livestock which helped to feed that population.

11
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:41,247
The Romans left in 410, at the beginning of the fifth century,

12
00:00:41,247 --> 00:00:47,349
and from then onwards the country suffered a series of invasions by tribes from present-day Germany

13
00:00:47,349 --> 00:00:53,070
and Denmark, the Angles, Saxons and Jutes, many of whom settled in the East End.

14
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:56,600
The technology they introduced meant that metal

15
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,330
and leather goods were produced there for the first time.

16
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:02,180
And as the East End was by the river

17
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:05,540
ships could transport goods between there and foreign markets.

18
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:10,706
In the eleventh century, in 1066 to be precise, the Normans conquered England,

19
00:01:10,706 --> 00:01:16,060
and during the next few centuries London became one of the most powerful

20
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:17,860
and prosperous cities in Europe.

21
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:20,740
The East End benefited from this

22
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:24,668
and because there were fewer restrictions there than in the city itself,

23
00:01:24,668 --> 00:01:27,315
plenty of newcomers settled there from abroad,

24
00:01:27,315 --> 00:01:29,240
bringing their skills as workers

25
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:32,610
merchants or money-lenders during the next few hundred years.

26
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:38,373
In the sixteenth century the first dock was dug where ships were constructed,

27
00:01:38,373 --> 00:01:43,250
eventually making the East End the focus of massive international trade.

28
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,680
And in the late sixteenth century

29
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:48,329
when much of the rest of England was suffering economically,

30
00:01:48,329 --> 00:01:53,090
a lot of agricultural workers came to the East End to look for alternative work.

31
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:59,960
In the seventeenth century, the East End was still a series of separate, semi-rural settlements.

32
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,958
There was a shortage of accommodation,

33
00:02:01,958 --> 00:02:07,620
so marshland was drained and built on to house the large numbers of people now living there.

34
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:12,150
By the nineteenth century London was the busiest port in the world,

35
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:16,680
and this became the main source of employment in the East End.

36
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:21,102
Those who could afford to live in more pleasant surroundings moved out,

37
00:02:21,102 --> 00:02:26,629
and the area became one where the vast majority of people lived in extreme poverty,

38
00:02:26,629 --> 00:02:28,840
and suffered from appalling sanitary conditions.

39
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:33,200
That brief outline takes us to the beginning of the twentieth century

40
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,390
and now we'll turn to housing.

41
00:02:35,390 --> 00:02:37,604
At the beginning of the century,

42
00:02:37,604 --> 00:02:43,140
living conditions for the majority of working people in East London were very basic indeed.

43
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:48,100
Houses were crowded closely together and usually very badly built

44
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:49,790
because there was no regulation.

45
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:54,040
But the poor and needy were attracted by the possibility of work

46
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:55,680
and they had to be housed.

47
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,354
It was the availability, rather than the condition,

48
00:02:58,354 --> 00:03:02,700
of the housing that was the major concern for tenants and landlords alike.

49
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:05,248
Few houses had electricity at this time,

50
00:03:05,248 --> 00:03:08,480
so other sources of power were used

51
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:11,800
like coal for the fires which heated perhaps just one room.

52
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:16,443
Of course, the smoke from these contributed a great deal to the air pollution for

53
00:03:16,443 --> 00:03:18,300
which London used to be famous.

54
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:25,930
A tiny, damp, unhealthy house like this might well be occupied by two full families,

55
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:30,000
possibly including several children, grandparents, aunts and uncles.

56
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,860
Now, before I go on to health implications of this way of life

57
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:37,640
I'll say something about food and nutrition.
